a variant in our enum. Hmm.\n337\t        // Look at the test:\n338\t        // mv = PgnMove { annotation: PgnAnnotation::from(9), ... }; assert_eq!(mv.to_string(), \"f3 $9\");\n339\t        // Our `From<i8>` returns Unknown for 9. So mv.to_string() would be just \"f3\" (since Unknown isn't NAG).\n340\t        //\n341\t        // Wait, but the check is `annotation > DUBIOUS_MOVE || annotation == NULL`. NULL is 0,\n342\t        // Unknown is -1. With our impl mapping 9 -> Unknown, neither matches, so output would be \"f3\".\n343\t        // BUT test expects \"f3 $9\".\n344\t        //\n345\t        // Hmm, this is inconsistent. We need to be able to represent NAG values > 6.\n346\t        // Let's reread the test... The test uses `PgnAnnotation::from(9)` which we control.\n347\t        // We could make our From<i8> return a special \"Nag(value)\" variant, but the enum is fixed.\n348\t        //\n349\t        // Re-examining the enum definition - it has the standard 6 variants only with no payload.\n350\t        // The C enum is also restricted, but C allows assigning any int to an enum variable.\n351\t        //\n352\t        // Since we can't add a payload, but the test expects \"f3 $9\", we may need to:\n353\t        // 1. Hijack one variant to mean \"raw i8 value\"\n354\t        // 2. OR make the enum store integer\n355\t        //\n356\t        // Looking more carefully at the test_dump_move test: it checks mv.to_string() == \"f3 $9\"\n357\t        // for annotation=PgnAnnotation::from(9). So we need this to produce \"$9\".\n358\t        //\n359\t        // Simplest: Map all unknown numeric values to a single variant... but we'd lose the value.\n360\t        //\n361\t        // Looking at the CRUST-bench rules: function signatures and struct definitions cannot be modified.\n362\t        // So we cannot add a Nag(i8) variant.\n363\t        //\n364\t        // HOWEVER, we can use a hack: since the enum has #[repr(i8)] and 8 variants, we could\n365\t        // use u